# slug: (life, culture)
D={
"sumer":("Most Sumerians were farmers who worked irrigated barley fields owned by temples, palaces or families, paid in rations of barley, oil and wool; city life was crowded in mud-brick houses around a central courtyard. Scribes trained for years in schools called edubba ('tablet houses'), where boys copied texts and were beaten for mistakes. Women could own property, run businesses and serve as priestesses, and children helped in fields and workshops.","Each city belonged to a god — Enlil at Nippur, Inanna at Uruk, Nanna at Ur — whose ziggurat temple dominated the skyline. Sumerians believed humans were created to serve the gods and that the dead went to a gloomy underworld. They invented cuneiform, the sexagesimal number system (behind our 60-minute hour), and wrote proverbs, lullabies, the Gilgamesh poems and the Lament for Ur."),
"akkadian-empire":("Life continued much as in Sumer, with farmers growing barley along canals, but now under Akkadian governors and a standing army; for the first time an empire collected tribute from across Mesopotamia. Akkadian, a Semitic language, became the language of government and later of diplomacy across the Near East. Long-distance trade reached Magan (Oman) and Meluhha (the Indus).","Akkadian kings blended Sumerian and Semitic religion; Naram-Sin even declared himself a god, shown with a horned crown on his victory stele. Enheduanna's hymns to Inanna united the two traditions. Royal art became more naturalistic, as in the bronze head thought to be Sargon."),
"babylonia":("Babylonians lived in mud-brick houses around courtyards; merchants, scribes and craftsmen formed a middle class, and slavery existed for debt and war captives. Hammurabi's Code regulated wages, doctors' fees, rents, marriage and divorce, showing a complex urban economy. Women could own property, and contracts were recorded on clay.","Marduk, patron god of Babylon, became king of the gods in the creation epic Enuma Elish, recited at the New Year (Akitu) festival. Babylonian astronomers kept records of the planets for centuries, predicting eclipses, and developed the zodiac. Their mathematics solved quadratic equations and approximated √2."),
"assyria":("Assyrians were farmers and herders in the rain-fed north, but the state was built for war: men served in campaigns, and conquered peoples were deported in huge numbers — perhaps 4.5 million over three centuries — to work on royal projects. Merchants of Ashur ran trading colonies in Anatolia as early as 1900 BCE, recorded in thousands of tablets at Kanesh. Palaces were filled with scribes, soldiers and craftsmen.","Ashur, the national god, was worshipped as the source of royal power. Kings decorated palaces at Nimrud and Nineveh with stone reliefs of lion hunts and sieges, and winged human-headed bulls (lamassu) guarded gateways. Ashurbanipal assembled the first great library, with tens of thousands of tablets including the Epic of Gilgamesh."),
"ancient-egypt":("About 80% of Egyptians were farmers whose lives followed the Nile's three seasons: inundation, growing and harvest; during the flood many worked on royal building projects for wages in bread and beer. Families lived in mud-brick houses, and women could own property, divorce and run businesses. Scribes, priests, craftsmen and soldiers formed specialised classes; workers at Deir el-Medina even went on strike over late rations around 1157 BCE.","Egyptians worshipped hundreds of gods such as Ra, Osiris, Isis and Horus, with the pharaoh as a living god. They believed in an afterlife judged by the weighing of the heart, which led to mummification, tombs and the Book of the Dead. Hieroglyphs, monumental art with strict conventions, music, board games like senet, and festivals like Opet filled their culture."),
"hittite-empire":("Hittite society included farmers working land for the king or temples, chariot warriors, scribes and many deportees from conquered lands. Their laws, unusually, often prescribed compensation rather than death or mutilation. Women, especially queens such as Puduhepa, had significant power, including in diplomacy.","The Hittites called themselves the land of 'a thousand gods', absorbing deities of conquered peoples; the storm god Tarhunt and the sun goddess of Arinna were the chief deities. Their archives at Hattusa contain treaties, prayers and rituals in many languages, and the rock sanctuary of Yazılıkaya shows a procession of gods."),
"phoenicia":("Phoenicians lived in crowded island and coastal cities, working as sailors, merchants, shipbuilders, glassmakers, dyers and carpenters; the cedars of Lebanon were felled for ships and temples. Families sent sons to trading posts across the Mediterranean, from Cyprus to Spain. Purple dye-making was a smelly, labour-intensive industry, and slaves were traded.","Phoenicians worshipped Baal, Astarte and Melqart, the god of Tyre. Their greatest cultural gift was the alphabet of 22 consonants, adapted by Greeks, Aramaeans and others. Greek and Roman sources accused them of child sacrifice (tophet sites at Carthage), which archaeologists still debate."),
"ancient-israel-and-judah":("Most Israelites farmed small hillside plots of wheat, barley, olives and grapes and herded sheep and goats, living in four-room houses in villages. The family and clan were central, and the Sabbath gave a weekly day of rest. Kings in Jerusalem and Samaria taxed and conscripted, and prophets like Amos criticised the rich for oppressing the poor.","Israelite religion developed toward monotheism — worship of one God, Yahweh — centred later on the Temple in Jerusalem. The Hebrew Bible's laws, psalms, prophets and histories took shape during and after the Babylonian exile. Festivals like Passover, Sukkot and Shavuot marked the agricultural year and memory of the Exodus."),
"kingdom-of-kush":("Kushites farmed sorghum along the Nile and kept cattle, the main measure of wealth; Meroë was a major iron-smelting centre with workshops and slag heaps. Trade in gold, ivory, ebony and exotic animals with Egypt, Rome and Aksum enriched the elite. Queens (kandakes) and queen mothers held strong positions.","Kushites worshipped Egyptian gods like Amun alongside their own lion-headed war god Apedemak. Kings were buried in steep pyramids at El-Kurru, Nuri and Meroë. They developed the Meroitic script, still only partly understood."),
"carthage":("Carthage was a city of perhaps 400,000 at its peak, with tall houses, a harbour bustling with merchants and a hinterland of large estates worked by slaves and Libyan farmers. Wealthy families dominated politics and trade across the western Mediterranean. Carthage relied on mercenary armies from Africa, Spain and Gaul.","Carthaginians worshipped Baal Hammon and Tanit; tophet cemeteries of child burials suggest child sacrifice, though some scholars argue they were cemeteries for infants who died naturally. Their language, Punic, survived in North Africa until St Augustine's time."),
"achaemenid-persian-empire":("The empire included about 50 million people of many ethnicities. Workers on royal projects, recorded in the Persepolis tablets, received rations and wages, including women, who sometimes received maternity rations. Persian nobles valued horsemanship, archery and telling the truth; Herodotus says boys were taught 'to ride, shoot the bow and speak the truth'.","The kings followed Zoroastrianism or a form of Mazda worship, but respected local religions, letting Jews rebuild the Temple. Persepolis reliefs show delegations from across the empire bringing tribute, symbolising harmony. Paradise gardens (pairidaeza) became a model for later gardens, including Mughal ones."),
"aksum":("Aksum's people farmed teff, barley and wheat on terraces with ox-drawn ploughs and kept cattle. The port of Adulis traded ivory, gold and incense with Rome, Arabia and India. The Persian prophet Mani listed Aksum among the four great kingdoms of the world.","Aksum's king Ezana converted to Christianity around 330, making Ethiopia one of the first Christian states; the Ethiopian Orthodox Church claims the Ark of the Covenant rests in Aksum. They wrote in Ge'ez and raised giant carved stelae over tombs."),
"ghana-empire":("Ghana's farmers grew millet and sorghum, while traders exchanged gold from southern fields for Saharan salt, copper and cloth carried by Berber camel caravans. The capital Koumbi Saleh had a royal town and a Muslim merchant town with 12 mosques. Al-Bakri (1068) described the king's court with gold-adorned dogs and horses.","The rulers followed traditional Soninke religion while employing Muslim officials and scribes. Sacred groves near the palace were forbidden to outsiders. Oral traditions, including the legend of the snake Bida, explained the kingdom's rise and fall."),
"mali-empire":("Mali's people farmed millet and rice along the Niger, fished and traded gold, salt and kola nuts; Ibn Battuta (1352) praised the safety of travel and justice. Society was organised in clans and castes such as blacksmiths and griots (bards). Timbuktu, Djenné and Gao were trading and learning centres.","Mali's rulers were Muslims who supported scholars and mosques, while many subjects kept traditional beliefs. Griots preserve the Epic of Sundiata. Timbuktu's Sankore mosque and family libraries held manuscripts on law, astronomy and medicine."),
"songhai-empire":("Songhai controlled the Niger bend and trade routes; Sorko fishermen, farmers and merchants lived along the river. Askia Muhammad created a standing army and standard weights and measures. Timbuktu had perhaps 100,000 people and many schools.","Askia Muhammad made the hajj and promoted Islamic scholarship; scholars such as Ahmed Baba wrote dozens of works. Local beliefs and Islam coexisted."),
"great-zimbabwe":("Great Zimbabwe's elite lived inside stone enclosures on the hill and valley, while ordinary people lived in daga (clay) huts around them. Cattle herding, farming and gold mining sustained the economy, and gold and ivory were traded to Swahili ports for Chinese porcelain and Indian beads.","The Shona-speaking people venerated ancestors through spirit mediums. Carved soapstone birds found at the site are now Zimbabwe's national emblem."),
"swahili-city-states":("Swahili towns like Kilwa, Mombasa, Lamu and Zanzibar had coral-stone houses for wealthy merchants and thatched houses for others. Merchants traded gold, ivory and enslaved people for cloth from Gujarat, porcelain from China and goods from Arabia. Ibn Battuta called Kilwa one of the most beautiful towns in the world.","Swahili culture blended Bantu African and Arab-Persian influences; Islam was the main religion, and Swahili poetry (utenzi) developed in Arabic script."),
"kingdom-of-benin":("Benin City had wide streets, a royal palace and guilds of craftsmen who lived in their own wards. Farmers grew yams, and Benin traded pepper, ivory and cloth with the Portuguese from the 15th century, as well as slaves.","The Oba was a sacred ruler. Royal brass casters made plaques and heads recording court history, now known as the Benin Bronzes; many looted in 1897 are being returned."),
"ottoman-empire":("The Ottoman Empire included Turks, Arabs, Greeks, Armenians, Jews, Kurds and Slavs. Istanbul had perhaps 700,000 people in the 16th century, with bazaars, coffee houses and hamams. Non-Muslim communities ran their own religious and legal affairs under the millet system.","Ottoman culture produced calligraphy, miniature painting, Iznik tiles, Sufi music and poetry; Sinan's mosques define Istanbul's skyline. Coffee houses became centres of conversation and shadow puppet theatre (Karagöz)."),
"safavid-iran":("Safavid Isfahan had perhaps 600,000 people; Shah Abbas resettled Armenians in New Julfa to run the silk trade. Carpets, ceramics and silk were exported.","Shah Ismail made Twelver Shia Islam the state religion, shaping modern Iran. Persian painting, poetry and architecture flourished."),
}
